342 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In this section, we will review information on the scale of gold mining in the three Kordofan states, as well as information on CSR, in order to determine the extent to which the proposed policies align with practical realities. This is done by presenting detailed figures from official state sources. To begin, the following table shows information on mining-waste companies and exploration licences granted to various companies in the three states:)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
343 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Table No. \(1\) Source \(Ministry of Minerals Documents\) 28)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
344 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
345 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The table also shows that mining has come to occupy extremely large areas of land in the Kordofan states, areas that represent some of the most heavily used and inhabited spaces in the region. Most of these allocations were made during the Bashir Government, which did not take free and informed public participation into account when making such decisions. This was done by withholding information and completing procedures centrally, without local consultation. This issue is particularly sensitive in areas where land is held under collective customary ownership, and where communities already face resource scarcity and long-standing disputes over land, whether water sources, grazing areas, or agricultural land. Mining therefore adds an entirely new layer of conflict in these settings.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
